        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>Objectives:<br />
The purpose of the Spaceflight Cognitive Assessment Tool for the Lunar-Mars Life Support Test Project (LMLSTP) Phase III Test was to test an objective cognitive assessment tool in an environment comparable to a space station. This tool was called the Spaceflight Cognitive Assessment Tool (S-CAT) and was expected to assist in decisions regarding the cognitive health and capabilities of a crew. In addition to its monitoring functions, it could also assist in the case of accident, injury or off-nominal environmental exposure.</p>
<p>The two specific aims were to evaluate the use and sensitivity of the test during the LMLSTP study and to provide measures of cognitive functions that could assist in recommendations for the crew during the Phase III mission.</p>
<p>Three important operational issues regarding the use of the S-CAT were to be considered. First, subjects needed to be proficient at performing the test, so the amount of training and practice required to alleviate task-learning effects was to be determined. Based on previous testing experience, the S-CAT development team predicted that five to ten sessions would ensure that the subject learned the tasks well enough to work at the best performance level. Because the Phase III subjects did not have the time required for this recommendation, one familiarization and four sessions for training and baseline data collection sufficed. The second main issue concerned the best way to control the S-CAT data. Poor performance on the S-CAT would indicate a neurocognitive problem and possibly jeopardize the career of the subject. Thus, subjects could be reluctant to take the test or report the results. For LMLSTP Phase III, the best option was to trust, but not require, the crewmembers to report anomalies on the routine tests. The third issue was how often the S-CAT should be scheduled. Subjects needed to take the test often enough to keep up the skills required for it. The test would be administered if an event occurred that could impair cognitive function, and, for the scores to be meaningful, subjects must be proficient at taking the test.</p>
<p>Earlier experiments have indicated that the space environment might adversely affect human cognitive capabilities. Both short- and long-duration missions have provided some possible evidence of mild degradations in the crewmembers ability to remember tasks and recall information. In addition, off-nominal conditions have been shown to affect a person's ability to function. Experience in operations from the Space Station Mir indicated a need for cognitive and performance assessments.</p>
<p>The S-CAT was based on tests derived from the Automated Neuropsychological Assessment Metrics that were developed for the Department of Defense. The S-CAT consisted of several subtests. Code Substitution subtests tested memory and recall while a Running Memory subtest measured sustained concentration. A Math subtest measured verbal working memory and a Match-2-Sample subtest measured visual working memory. Due to the need for the test in a relatively short timeframe, the assessment tool was created from tests and methods already validated.</p>
<p>There were several constraints in the development of the S-CAT, which was intended to eventually be a part of ISS assessments. These constraints included the availability of computing equipment on the ISS, a limited amount of crew time to perform the test, and the need for results to be immediately fed back to the subject.</p>
<p>The S-CAT development team, which included clinical neuropsychologists and experts in the construction and use of automated psychological tests, developed a short and a long version of the S-CAT. The short version took approximately 15 minutes for a subject to complete and allowed monitoring of cognitive functions while providing initial diagnostic assistance in the event of an injury or toxin exposure. The long version of the test could be done in 35 minutes and was intended for enhanced diagnosis of the subject's condition.</p>
<p>Approach:<br />
Four prime and four back-up Phase III LMLSTP subjects were proficient at taking the S-CAT and provided baseline data. First, subjects took a short familiarization about the use and purpose of the S-CAT. They each completed four sessions of the short version of the test and two sessions of the long version for baseline data collection. The same protocol planned for Space Station Mir and ISS missions was followed. Subjects were scheduled to take at least the short version of the test once per month during the mission for a total of three sessions. The subjects were responsible for scheduling and performing the test. The long version of the S-CAT was planned for a total of two times during the Phase III test, suggested at approximately days 45 and 75. After Phase III ended, the subjects were scheduled to be debriefed and possibly take the short version of the S-CAT one more time.</p>
<p>Taking the S-CAT repeatedly ensured that there would be good data from pretest and nominal operations over time for comparisons with any mishaps. Subjects would also maintain proficiency on the tests so that relearning effects would not affect the data and lead to a misdiagnosis of the cognitive condition. Although the LMLSTP subjects were given suggested test days of 30, 60 and 88 days of the mission, they were given the discretion to choose the exact days to complete the test. To address subject concern of reporting any low test scores, the subjects were given the freedom to decide whether to share the results. This approach mitigated the fear of detrimental judgment from management or the medical monitor, who did retain the right to request the results if warranted.</p>
<p>The hardware consisted of one DOS-based notebook computer and one desktop computer with a DOS-based operating system required to run the S-CAT. During the Phase III mission, the prime crew used their personal desktop computers in the test chamber, and the back-up crewmembers used a notebook computer stored in the test control room.</p>
<p>Results:<br />
The long version of the S-CAT was troublesome due to some imperfections in the software. No meaningful baseline or in-test data were collected from the long version of the S-CAT. Significant improvements to the S-CAT have been made overall due to the resolution of these problems and other software-related issues.</p>
<p>The group training was not optimal. Each subject should have been trained in a private setting free from distractions. The first two pretest data collection sessions may not have produced the same quality of results as sessions that would have been performed in a more private environment. Direct observations and subject comments showed that the S-CAT needed to be performed in a quiet location without interruptions. The back-up crewmembers also had difficulty taking the test due to the constant distractions of on-going communications, problem solving and visitors in the control room.</p>
<p>For each subject, performance on the short version of the S-CAT leveled off, indicating proficiency, after the familiarization and two data collection sessions. It was inconclusive when the performance leveled off for the long version due to the software problems. The number of sessions required to achieve proficiency appeared to be fewer than the test developers originally predicted.</p>
<p>The S-CAT appeared to measure cognition reliably based on preliminary analysis of the data, and further assessment of the test was warranted with validation studies. No off-nominal events such as head trauma or exposure to toxins occurred during the mission, so evaluation of the comparison of such adverse conditions to normal conditions could not be made. In addition, control of the data and its release to the medical monitor by a subject was not tested. Conditions upon which a subject could have been concerned by adverse judgment did not occur. It was decided to allow the subjects to maintain control of their test results, unless an off-nominal event occurred. In that case, data could be used for medically-based diagnostic and management recommendations.</p>
<p>Neither the short version nor the long version of the S-CAT was taken on the days recommended, and some subjects did not complete either version for the recommended number of times. Those who did complete the test followed no schedule. When subjects were reminded to take the S-CAT, they did not necessarily fit it into their schedules, even though they attempted to complete the test. Futhermore, no meaningful data were collected from the S-CAT taken by the back-up LMLSTP subjects, although one or two of them attempted to take the short version once or twice.</p>
<p>Recommendations from nearly all subjects, as well as the history of manned space operations, indicated that the S-CAT sessions must be a requirement set in a timelined schedule and not simply recommended for suggested days. The test should also be scheduled during the normal workday so the subject is working at normal conditions rather than those under time and energy constraints. Following these recommendations should ensure that meaningful data can be collected from the S-CAT and, more importantly, that data is available for use in an event that is suspected of impairing cognition. Overall results showed that a computer-based cognitive assessment tool could be used on the Space Station Mir and on the ISS since such a tool was successfully developed and tested in the 91-day chamber study.<end></end></p>
